    "section_summary": {
      "type": "string",
      "extract_value": "This section describes the 12-item Body Image-Acceptance and Action Questionnaire (BI-AAQ) used to measure body image flexibility, its 7-point response scale, and reverse-scoring so higher scores indicate greater flexibility. It reports internal consistency (alpha = .92) in the present sample and cites prior mean BI-AAQ scores in U.S. college students (66.6, SD=15.1), Portuguese adults (67.4, SD=12.0), and a small eating-disorder patient sample (31.4, SD=1.8). The section does not report country, sample size, survey method, BMI, or correlations with body appreciation.",
      "evidence_source": "Participants completed the 12-item Body Image-Acceptance and Action Questionnaire (BI-AAQ; Sandoz et al., 2013) as a measure of body image flexibility. In the present sample, an alpha of .92 was achieved. Mean scores of 66.6 (SD=15.1) and 67.4 (SD=12.0) were reported in non-clinical samples of U.S. college students (Sandoz et al., 2013) and Portuguese adults (Ferreira et al., 2011) respectively. Ferreira et al. (2011) additionally observed an average BI-AAQ score of 31.4 (SD=1.8) in a small sample of eating disorder patients.",
